22:03:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A belief being widespread or passed down through generations does not make it reliable evidence. Many ideas have persisted because of fear, wartime propaganda, colonial stereotypes, political messaging, or limited contact with the people being described—not because they accurately characterize an entire population. It is understandable that repeated stories, family views, and dramatic news coverage can make a pattern feel obvious. But that is different from testing the claim fairly. A fair test would compare: - rates of violence across specific countries and time periods; - the role of war, state instability, poverty, repression, and armed conflict; - whether violence is committed by a broad population or by small militant organizations; - who is most often harmed by those organizations; and - the actual risk faced by an ordinary visitor in a particular city. On those measures, “Muslims are more violent” is far too broad and unsupported to be useful. Muslim-majority societies range from highly safe and stable to places experiencing serious conflict, just as Christian-, Hindu-, Buddhist-, Jewish-, and nonreligious-majority societies do. Individuals cannot reasonably be judged from the actions of an extremist minority. You do not have to deny extremist violence or pretend that every destination has the same risk. It is reasonable to avoid active conflict zones, heed terrorism advisories, and take security precautions. The key is to base those decisions on credible, current, location-specific information—not inherited assumptions about a religion or its followers.
