22:09:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Those attacks were real, horrific, and several were carried out by people motivated by jihadist extremist ideology. It is reasonable to recognize that Islamist extremism has been a recurring terrorism threat in parts of Europe and that authorities need to address it seriously. But the pattern is not “the same religion every single time.” It is a pattern involving particular **violent ideological movements**—often ISIS-inspired or al-Qaeda-linked networks—within a much larger population of Muslims who neither support nor participate in violence. The perpetrators represent a tiny fraction of Muslims in France, Germany, and the UK, and Muslims have also been among the victims of such attacks. It is also important not to select only one category of attack. Europe has faced terrorist violence from far-right extremists, ethno-nationalist and separatist groups, anti-government extremists, and other actors as well. The risk profile changes over time and by country. The accurate safety conclusion is: - Jihadist extremism is a real, recurring threat that merits monitoring. - Specific plots, networks, venues, and threat alerts should be taken seriously. - Muslim identity alone is not a meaningful predictor of whether someone is violent, nor does a Muslim-majority country automatically carry the same risk. For a traveler, current advisories and local threat reporting are far more useful than trying to infer danger from a population’s religion.
